  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Federico Boccaccini's <em>Lotze et son h\u00e9ritage</em> (2015) has generated four citations across European and Latin American scholarship since publication. The work circulates in academic libraries and contributes to ongoing philosophical discourse on Hermann Lotze's theoretical legacy, particularly regarding epistemology, idealism, and the philosophy of psychology. Recent engagement includes a 2025 article from Chile examining objectification and validity in Lotze's theoretical philosophy, demonstrating sustained international interest in the book's interpretive framework.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Peter Lang in 2015 as part of the <em>Philosophie et Politique / Philosophy and Politics</em> series (ISSN 1376-0920), this scholarly monograph addresses Hermann Lotze's philosophical inheritance and remains relevant within German idealism and history of 19th-century philosophy. The work is held in at least four library collections globally, indicating modest but steady institutional adoption as a reference source in philosophy education and research.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record reveals a geographically distributed scholarly audience. Between 2017 and 2025, the book was cited once in a bibliographic chapter (Germany), once in an introduction to Lotze's philosophy of psychology (Paderborn University), and twice in journal articles\u2014one addressing reconciliation of idealism and realism after Hegel (France, 2022) and another examining objectification and validity in Lotze's theory (Chile, 2025). This pattern reflects engagement across German-language, Francophone, and Spanish-language philosophical communities, with particular concentration in academic institutions active in history of modern philosophy.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing works span both monographic and journal contexts, with emphasis on historical and epistemological questions central to Lotze's theoretical system. Authors including Nikolay Milkov, Charlotte Morel, and Jacinto P\u00e1ez Bonifaci have built on Boccaccini's interpretive work when addressing the status of objectivity, consciousness, and idealist-realist dialectics in 19th-century European thought. The most recent 2025 citation suggests that Boccaccini's analysis continues to inform contemporary scholarly debate on Lotze's legacy in theoretical philosophy.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Although event-based attention data are not available, the work's presence in established academic publishing channels, library systems, and the peer-reviewed citing literature indicates functional circulation within its intended disciplinary audience. The pattern of uptake\u2014sparse but substantive\u2014is consistent with specialized historical philosophy scholarship aimed at an expert readership rather than general readership.</p>",
